		{
			"id": "EyzcNFGLFNzdzfwQp",
			"description": "Power Armour, Adepta Sororitas",
			"local_notes": "Weak Points 5: when a hit on this armour fails to penetrate, roll 3d; on 5 or less it found a gap and DR is halved against it.\n\nBuilt and rigged for an unaugmented human body: no black carapace or other neural interface is required, and a Sister of Battle suffers none of the fit/control penalties a non-Astartes wearer would face in Astartes plate. Rigid heavy armour DX/Stealth penalties apply; treat as one step lighter than Astartes plate for encumbrance purposes.\n\nLore: Adepta Sororitas power armour is consistently described (Codex Adepta Sororitas background, Black Library's Sisters of Battle fiction such as Ephrael Stern: The Heretic Saint) as true powered plate — lighter and less overbuilt than Astartes armour, because it's engineered from the outset for a baseline (if exceptionally devout and disciplined) human body rather than a post-human one.\n\nDesign: Torso DR 85, the framework's Sororitas anchor: raised from 70 when carapace went to 70, so power armour stays clearly above the best unpowered plate while remaining well under Astartes plate (100). Exact odds per hit: bolter 45%, hellgun 8d(3) 46%, plasma 100%. Limbs 64, neck 69, skull 80, face 53, feet 42, hands 38, eye 30 (lowered for the Weak Points and eye-lens rule; see framework.md), keeping the earlier location ratios. Weight and cost are reasoned: the assembled suit is 80 lb and $52,000 (shell 53 lb plus subsystems), about a third of an Astartes suit's weight at a fraction of the price, since it needs no genetic modification of its wearer. GCS adds a container's children to its own weight and cost, so the plate shell carries only its own remainder (13 lb, $8,000) and the children carry the rest (27 lb, $22,000).",
			"tags": [
				"Body Armor",
				"Imperium",
				"Adepta Sororitas"
			],
