				},
				{
					"id": "eAfmLgfetzkd0_wJh",
					"description": "Recyclers",
					"local_notes": "Reclaims water and scrubs air, extending sealed endurance to several days.\n\nLore: Rarely dwelt on in fiction but implied whenever characters in sealed plate operate for extended periods without resupply.\n\nDesign: Minor flavour subsystem, no mechanical bonus; present for completeness of the container breakdown.",
					"base_value": "1500",
					"base_weight": "3 lb",
					"quantity": 1,
					"equipped": true
				},
				{
					"id": "e7GkNz8br9_usqy9Z",
					"description": "Servo-Motor Actuators (Sororitas Pattern)",
					"local_notes": "Powered assistance sized for a human frame; noticeably weaker than an Astartes suit's motors.\n\nLore: Sisters of Battle are shown out-muscling unarmoured humans effortlessly but never matching a Space Marine's raw strength; the suit's motors reflect that ceiling.\n\nDesign: ST bonus set well below the Astartes figures (see Mark VII) to keep the human-scale Sororitas suit clearly weaker in raw power while still far above an unarmoured human, consistent with lore depictions.",
					"base_value": "14000",
					"base_weight": "14 lb",
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "lifting_st",
							"amount": 6
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "striking_st",
							"amount": 3
						}
					],
					"quantity": 1,
					"equipped": true
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "eW7QdI_Zn3LtaXpL-",
			"description": "Power Armour, Mark II (Crusade Pattern)",
			"local_notes": "Weak Points 6: when a hit on this armour fails to penetrate, roll 3d; on 6 or less it found a gap and DR is halved against it.\n\nRequires the black carapace interface to move and fight at full efficiency (see design note). Early plate with poorer seals and joints than later marks (Weak Points 6). Rigid heavy armour DX/Stealth penalties apply.\n\nLore: Mark II \"Crusade Armour\" was the first pattern produced in the numbers needed to arm the Great Crusade's expanding Legions: simplified, mass-producible plate that replaced the ornate, hand-fitted Mark I suits. Horus Heresy-era Black Library fiction (the Horus Heresy novel series) depicts it as solid but unremarkable — the workhorse suit of the early Crusade, superseded once heavier and then more mobile patterns entered production.\n\nDesign: Torso DR 92, 8 below the Mark VII baseline (100), inside the framework's \"Marks vary ±10–15 around this\" spread and a little under Mark IV (95): an early, mass-produced pattern with poorer seals, hence Weak Points 6 against Mark IV's 5 (Mark VII is 4). Limbs use the Mark VII offsets from torso (arms and legs -12, face and feet -25, neck and hands -37). Eye lenses 40 as on every Astartes mark. Servo ST bonus (lifting 8, striking 4) a step under the Mark VII's, typical of the early suits. Cost $30,000, $10,000 under the Mark VII because it is the simpler, cheaper plate.",
