		{
			"id": "eBWSXA2AJUCH_h57W",
			"description": "Power Armour, Mark III (Iron Armour)",
			"local_notes": "Weak Points 4: when a hit on this armour fails to penetrate, roll 3d; on 4 or less it found a gap and DR is halved against it.\n\nHeaviest of the Heresy-era patterns; the design prioritises frontal protection over mobility (treat as an extra -1 to Dodge or an extra step of encumbrance at the GM's discretion, on top of the usual rigid heavy armour penalties). Requires the black carapace interface.\n\nLore: Mark III \"Iron Armour\" is described in Horus Heresy-era Black Library fiction and Forge World's Horus Heresy background as heavy, front-loaded plate designed for close assault and breaching actions — thick chest and shoulder plate at the cost of speed, issued for grinding, attritional fighting rather than manoeuvre warfare.\n\nDesign: Torso DR 113, 13 above the Mark VII baseline (100), near the top of the framework's \"±10–15\" spread, reflecting Mark III's reputation as the single heaviest mainline pattern. Limbs use the Mark VII offsets from torso (arms and legs -13, face and feet -25, neck and hands -38). Eye lenses 40 as on every Astartes mark. The extra bulk is also flagged qualitatively in `notes` as a mobility cost, since the DR anchor spread has no separate mechanism for encoding \"heavier but slower\" beyond the number itself.",
